$13! = 6227020800$
$14! = 87178291200$
$15! = 1307674368000$
$16! = 20922789888000$
$17! = 355687428096000$
$18! = 6402373705728000$
$19! = 121645100408832000$
$20! = 2432902008176640000$
$21! = 51090942171709440000$ (approx $5 \times 10^{19}$)

The maximum value of $X$ is $3 \times 10^{18}$.
Since $20! \approx 2.4 \times 10^{18}$ and $21! \approx 5.1 \times 10^{19}$, the maximum possible value for $N$ is 20.
Wait, let me double check $21!$.
$20! = 2,432,902,008,176,640,000$.
$21 \times 20! \approx 21 \times 2.4 \times 10^{18} \approx 50 \times 10^{18} = 5 \times 10^{19}$.
The constraint is $X \le 3 \times 10^{18}$.
So $N$ will be at most 20.
Actually, let's check $18!$.
$18! = 6,402,373,705,728,000 \approx 6.4 \times 10^{15}$.
$19! = 121,645,100,408,832,000 \approx 1.2 \times 10^{17}$.
$20! = 2,432,902,008,176,640,000 \approx 2.4 \times 10^{18}$.
$21! = 51,090,942,171,709,440,000 \approx 5.1 \times 10^{19}$.
So indeed, $N$ is between 1 and 20.

Since the range of $N$ is very small (1 to 20), we can simply precompute factorials or compute them on the fly and compare with $X$.
Alternatively, since $N$ is small, we can iterate $N$ starting from 1, computing $N!$ and checking if it equals $X$. If it does, print $N$ and exit. If $N! > X$, then something is wrong (but the problem guarantees a solution exists).

Algorithm:
1. Read $X$ from standard input.
2. Initialize $n = 1$ and $fact = 1$.
3. Loop:
    a. If $fact == X$, print $n$ and terminate.

